copy_tiles(): compute the tile hint width before overwriting the framebuffer

The nt == 1 branch of copy_tiles() is meant to narrow a changed tile's update
rectangle to the columns that actually differ ("optimization for tall skinny
lines, e.g. wm frame ... could help for a slow link"), but it ran *after* the
memcpy() that copies the new tile data into main_fb, comparing s_dst against
s_src over exactly the range memcpy() had just made identical.  memcmp()
therefore never reported a difference, first_x/last_x stayed -1 for every tile,
and hint_updates() took its `if (tx < 0)` fallback and sent the full tile width
every time.  The optimization has never had any effect.

Move the scan ahead of the memcpy() so it sees the old and new contents, and
replace the per-pixel memcmp() loop with a byte scan that only examines the
pixels that can still widen [first_x, last_x] - once the range spans the tile
there is nothing left to learn.  memcmp() with a run-time length is never
inlined, so the original cost one PLT call per pixel per line.

first_diff_byte()/last_diff_byte() use SSE2 when the compiler advertises it
(baseline on amd64) and a portable 64-bit word scan otherwise.  Neither reads
outside [0,n): the 16-byte step is guarded by i+16<=n and the 8-byte step by
i+8<=n, with a byte-wise remainder.

Verified against the original loop over 400k randomized cases (pixelsize 1/2/4,
widths 1..32), with and without SSE2.  End to end against a scrolling 2px-wide
line, incremental traffic drops from 552,961 to 34,561 pixels while the client's
reconstructed framebuffer stays byte-identical to a fresh full-screen capture.

Not yet submitted upstream.

